WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.600
How can light have momentum if it doesn’t have mass?

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:05.080
Momentum does not require mass!

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:12.460
This episode was made possible by generous supporters on Patreon.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:13.160
Hey Crazies.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:16.960
I get these comments all the time and I’m tired of responding individually.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.510
I’m making a video instead!

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.650
Ok, so, momentum.

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:23.500
The first time students see it, it’s written like this.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:27.000
The "v" stands for velocity and the "m" stands for mass.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.080
The "p" is short for a Latin word meaning "to go to" or seek.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.140
It’s a nice simple relationship.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:37.000
You can have more momentum by moving faster or by having more mass.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.540
But momentum isn’t really relevant unless there are at least two objects.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:45.160
It only matters for these cars if they run into each other.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.680
That’s something you might call a collision for obvious reasons.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:53.760
We even categorize them based on how much kinetic energy is retained during the collision.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.560
Unfortunately, this gives students all sorts of wrong impressions.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.240
Misconception number one!

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:03.260
These so-called collisions don’t require physical contact.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:06.580
All that matters is that the objects exchange forces.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:09.600
Sure, this collision involved physical contact.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.360
But there are many forces in this universe and not all of them require contact,

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.700
like Newtonian gravity for example.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:19.620
Orbits are a kind of continuous collision.

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:24.220
You can model this motion using momentum exchanges and it works.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:29.260
Besides, if we want to get super technical, there’s no such thing as physical contact.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.340
At least in the way most people understand it.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:36.420
All contact forces are just complex manifestations of the electromagnetic force.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:37.940
Are you pushing on something?

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:42.140
That’s just electric repulsion between atoms on the outside of two solids.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:43.340
Pulling on something?

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:46.800
That’s just electric attraction between molecules in a solid.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:47.880
Buoyant force?

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.320
That’s electric repulsion again, but in fluids instead of solids.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:52.160
Friction?

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.820
Actually, we’re still trying to work out some of the details on that one.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.800
My point is the word "collision" is a very misleading word.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.500
They’re interactions, not collisions.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.140
Misconception number two!

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:05.760
Momentum does not require mass.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.640
This equation is not a fundamental definition.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:12.600
It’s a special case for slow moving objects with rest mass.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.580
It’s pretty good for things with speeds less than about 10% the speed of light.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:20.280
Any faster than that and you’ll need the version from special relativity.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:23.400
This gamma factor adjusts it so it’s more accurate.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.860
Even then, it still only works for objects with rest mass.

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:29.520
Surprise! Light doesn’t have any of that.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.200
So even this equation fails for light.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.080
That doesn’t mean that light has rest mass.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:35.900
It means the equation sucks.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:38.280
The one for light looks like this.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.200
But, hold up a second.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:44.720
If we really want to understand how light can have momentum, we need to answer a better question:

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:47.080
Um... what the heck is momentum?

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:48.500
That’s the one!

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:52.200
When these cars run into each other, they each exert a force on the other.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:56.160
With Newton’s second law, we could plug in a mass and get an acceleration.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.900
That changes the speeds of each car for as long as that force is exerted,

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:01.820
which is mainly just during the collision.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:03.940
So can light exert forces on things?

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.380
Oh yes, it most definitely can,

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:09.600
although not nearly as much as an object with rest mass.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.360
There is a force on the face of paper from the light in this room.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.660
It’s on the order of nano-newtons.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:20.300
If you’ve got a really big reflector though like a solar sail, the force can add up.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:23.680
The light hits the back of the sail and pushes it forward.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.200
If we use Newton’s second law again... wait a minute!

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:27.780
Light doesn’t have rest mass.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:28.900
This isn’t going to work.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:32.680
If I’m being completely honest though, that isn’t even Newton’s second law.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:35.460
Sure, this is the first version students ever see,

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:38.380
but it’s not actually about mass and acceleration.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:41.020
This is the real Newton’s second law.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:46.100
Any change in momentum for a single object tells us the force experienced by that object.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.240
So you can think of momentum as the oomph something has.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.480
Light hits the sail and reflects.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:54.080
The change in momentum results in a force.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:55.760
Light can exert forces.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:57.940
Therefore, light has momentum.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:03:59.280
No mass required!

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.080
Anyway, what was that third thing?

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:02.480
I think it was velocity.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:03.680
Yeah yeah yeah, that was it.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.300
Misconception number three!

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:08.220
Momentum doesn’t even require velocity.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:12.020
Light is just an electromagnetic wave, so if light has momentum,

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:15.460
then it stands to reason the electromagnetic field does too.

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:16.660
Hmm.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:18.680
Remember that weird Poynting vector?

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:21.660
It described the flow of energy in the electromagnetic field?

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:25.120
Well, we’ve already seen that momentum is related to energy for light,

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:28.520
which means this energy flow vector should also give us a momentum.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:31.440
That doesn’t even require the fields to be changing.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.700
Say we’ve got two big metal plates above and below carrying some charge.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.280
That gives us an electric field.

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:41.200
Now let’s say we’ve got two strong magnetic poles on either side.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:42.900
That gives us a magnetic field.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:47.260
The Poynting vector tells us that, as long as we’ve got both fields, we’ve got an energy flow

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:48.860
and so we’ve got a momentum.

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:51.980
That momentum points into your screen in this example.

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:55.440
There’s no moving matter here and the fields are static.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:57.120
Yet there is a momentum.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.560
And that momentum must be accounted for

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:02.840
at least if we expect conservation of momentum to hold true.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:05.300
So how can light have momentum if it doesn’t have mass?

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:07.460
Momentum does not require mass.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:09.480
It doesn’t even require velocity.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.460
This equation is not a fundamental definition.

00:05:12.469 --> 00:05:14.830
For fast moving objects, it’s this.

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:16.520
For light, it’s this.

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:20.740
All that really matters is that a change in momentum results in a force.

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:22.180
Light can exert a force.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:24.020
Therefore, light has momentum.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:25.240
End of story.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:28.640
If you’ve got any other questions about momentum, please ask in the comments.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:30.440
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:32.919
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like to keep up with us.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:35.920
Click that notification bell if you’re really into what we do here.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:39.620
